"{\n  \"entities\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Time zones\",\n      \"type\": \"Concept\",\n      \"description\": \"Time zones are regions of the Earth that observe the same standard time for legal, commercial, and social purposes. They are essential for organizing activities on a global scale and ensuring that everyone is on the same page when it comes to scheduling events and meetings.\",\n      \"attributes\": {\n        \"Number of Time Zones\": \"24\",\n        \"Span of Each Time Zone\": \"15 degrees of longitude\",\n        \"Reference Point\": \"Greenwich Mean Time (GMT)\",\n        \"Purpose\": \"Organizing activities on a global scale, ensuring coordination in scheduling events and meetings\",\n        \"Development Period\": \"Late 19th century to early 20th century\",\n        \"Evolution Driver\": \"Need for efficiency and coordination across vast distances, expansion of global trade and transportation networks\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"rail travel\",\n      \"type\": \"Activity\",\n      \"description\": \"Rail travel refers to the transportation of passengers and goods by trains, which became more widespread in the late 19th and early 20th centuries, influencing the practicality of the standard time zone system.\",\n      \"attributes\": {\n        \"Era of Expansion\": \"Late 19th and early 20th centuries\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"1847\",\n      \"type\": \"Year\",\n      \"description\": \"1847 is a year in the 19th century, during which significant developments in the standardization of time occurred, particularly in the context of railway schedules in Britain.\",\n      \"attributes\": {\n        \"Century\": \"19th Century\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"20th century\",\n      \"type\": \"Time Period\",\n      \"description\": \"The 20th century spans from the year 1901 to 2000 and is marked by significant technological, social, and cultural advancements.\",\n      \"attributes\": {\n        \"Start Year\": \"1901\",\n        \"End Year\": \"2000\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Sir Sandford Fleming\",\n      \"type\": \"Person\",\n      \"description\": \"Sir Sandford Fleming was a Canadian engineer who first proposed the concept of time zones in the late 19th century.\",\n      \"attributes\": {}\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"International Meridian Conference\",\n      \"type\": \"Event\",\n      \"description\": \"The International Meridian Conference was held in Washington, D.C. in 1884 to standardize time measurement and adopt a system of standard time zones based on GMT.\",\n      \"attributes\": {}\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Greenwich Meridian\",\n      \"type\": \"Geographical Location\",\n      \"description\": \"The Greenwich Meridian, located in London, is the Prime Meridian from which all time zones are calculated.\",\n      \"attributes\": {}\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"British Railway Companies\",\n      \"type\": \"Organization\",\n      \"description\": \"The British railway companies were among the first to implement a standardized time system in 1847, known as Railway Time.\",\n      \"attributes\": {}\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Daylight Saving Time (DST)\",\n      \"type\": \"Practice\",\n      \"description\": \"Daylight Saving Time is the practice of advancing clocks by one hour during the warmer months to extend daylight hours in the evening.\",\n      \"attributes\": {}\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Internet and Global Telecommunications Networks\",\n      \"type\": \"Technology\",\n      \"description\": \"The internet and global telecommunications networks have enabled instant communication and real-time collaboration across vast distances.\",\n      \"attributes\": {}\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Trains and Telegraphs\",\n      \"type\": \"Technology\",\n      \"description\": \"Trains and telegraphs were key technologies in the 19th century that highlighted the inefficiencies of using local time across long distances.\",\n      \"attributes\": {}\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Local Solar Time\",\n      \"type\": \"Time Measurement\",\n      \"description\": \"Local solar time is the time based on the position of the sun in the sky, which was used by communities before the advent of standardized time measurement.\",\n      \"attributes\": {}\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"United States\",\n      \"type\": \"Country\",\n      \"description\": \"The United States is a country that has implemented the time zone system, dividing the country into four main time zones: Eastern, Central, Mountain, and Pacific.\",\n      \"attributes\": {}\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Standard Time Zone System\",\n      \"type\": \"Time System\",\n      \"description\": \"A system of standard time zones based on Greenwich Mean Time (GMT) adopted in 1884 to facilitate global communication and coordination.\",\n      \"attributes\": {}\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Railway Time\",\n      \"type\": \"Time Standard\",\n      \"description\": \"Railway Time was a standard time adopted by British railway companies to synchronize their schedules.\",\n      \"attributes\": {}\n    }\n  ],\n  \"relations\": [\n    [\n      \"Time zones\",\n      \"Proposed By\",\n      \"Sir Sandford Fleming\",\n      \"Sir Sandford Fleming suggested dividing the world into 24 time zones, each one representing a one-hour difference in mean solar time.\"\n    ],\n    [\n      \"Time zones\",\n      \"Adopted At\",\n      \"International Meridian Conference\",\n      \"The concept of time zones gained traction and was adopted at the International Meridian Conference in 1884.\"\n    ],\n    [\n      \"Time zones\",\n      \"Reference Meridian\",\n      \"Greenwich Meridian\",\n      \"The Greenwich Meridian serves as the reference point (0 degrees longitude) for all time zones.\"\n    ],\n    [\n      \"Time zones\",\n      \"Implemented By\",\n      \"British Railway Companies\",\n      \"The British railway companies adopted a single standard time for their schedules, which was a precursor to the modern time zone system.\"\n    ],\n    [\n      \"Time zones\",\n      \"Refined By\",\n      \"Daylight Saving Time (DST)\",\n      \"The introduction of DST in the early 20th century further refined the use of time zones.\"\n    ],\n    [\n      \"Time zones\",\n      \"Facilitated By\",\n      \"Internet and Global Telecommunications Networks\",\n      \"These technologies have made coordinating time across different time zones easier than ever.\"\n    ],\n    [\n      \"Time zones\",\n      \"Highlighted Inefficiencies\",\n      \"Trains and Telegraphs\",\n      \"The need for time zones was driven by the inefficiencies in timekeeping that trains and telegraphs exposed.\"\n    ],\n    [\n      \"Time zones\",\n      \"Replaced\",\n      \"Local Solar Time\",\n      \"Time zones replaced the use of local solar time, which was previously set by each town or city.\"\n    ],\n    [\n      \"Time zones\",\n      \"Implemented In\",\n      \"United States\",\n      \"The United States adopted the time zone system and divided the country into four main time zones.\"\n    ],\n    [\n      \"rail travel\",\n      \"Influenced\",\n      \"Standard Time Zone System\",\n      \"The expansion of rail travel made the standard time zone system increasingly impractical due to the need for more precise and localized time measurements.\"\n    ],\n    [\n      \"1847\",\n      \"Year of Adoption\",\n      \"Railway Time\",\n      \"In 1847, British railway companies adopted Railway Time as a single standard time for their schedules.\"\n    ],\n    [\n      \"20th century\",\n      \"Introduced\",\n      \"Daylight Saving Time (DST)\",\n      \"The 20th century saw the introduction of Daylight Saving Time (DST) to further refine the system of time zones.\"\n    ]\n  ]\n}"